{
  "teamId": 116,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Detroit Tigers were a team in the earliest, most painful stages of a rebuild, and the season's story was never really about wins and losses — it was about survival, development, and the occasional flash of brightness in a very long year. Detroit finished 47-114, one of the worst records in modern franchise history, allowing 333 more runs than they scored and never seriously threatening .500 at any point after the calendar turned to May. The bullpen, anchored by veterans Joe Jiménez and Shane Greene, was a constant source of agony, with the team's highest-leverage moments far too often being home runs surrendered at the worst possible times.\n\nThe early weeks offered a sliver of hope. Christin Stewart's grand slam in April and Brandon Dixon's emerging pop gave the roster some personality, and the Tigers actually played respectable ball in March and April, going a combined 13-14. But May brought the collapse — a 9-19 month defined by Shane Greene surrendering a walk-off grand slam to Garrett Cooper and the bullpen repeatedly squandering close games. By the time June arrived, the season was effectively over in a competitive sense, with a 5-20 month cementing the Tigers' fate near the bottom of the American League.\n\nThe trade deadline brought the inevitable. Shane Greene, one of the few valuable trade chips on the roster, was shipped out, and with him went any pretense of competitiveness. July and August became a revolving door of roster experiments, with minor-league call-ups like Travis Demeritte, Ronny Rodriguez, and Willi Castro getting extended looks. Rodriguez's grand slam in late August was one of the few genuinely electric moments of the dog days, a reminder that individual brilliance could still surface even in the wreckage of a lost season.\n\nThe final chapter of the 2019 Tigers was less about baseball and more about auditions. September became a showcase for the next wave of Detroit talent, with Willi Castro and Victor Reyes flashing moments that suggested better days ahead. John Hicks' three-run home run in the season's final week — the single most win-probability-adding moment on the positive side of the ledger — was a fitting capstone: a career journeyman delivering one last meaningful swing for a franchise already looking toward 2020 and beyond. The 114 losses stung, but the seeds of a future rebuild had been quietly planted.",
